# TEMPLATE for the C# evaluation corpus. THIS FILE CARRIES NO ENTRIES, and the
# filled-in copy does not live in this repository.
#
# The manifest names the projects the engine is scored against and which set each
# is in. Publishing that invites the thing the held-out half exists to prevent: a
# rule tuned, however unconsciously, to the projects it will be measured on. The
# split still has to be pinned to be reproducible, so it is pinned in a file the
# operator holds. See manifest.sh, and CS_CORPUS_MANIFEST.
#
# Ten real projects, five designed against and five design-blind. The split is
# recorded HERE, in the tree, at pinned commits, because a split that lives in an
# agent's head is not a split and a rate measured against an unpinned corpus is not
# reproducible: upstream moves and a regression becomes indistinguishable from a
# dependency bump.
#
# ── FIELDS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   name  set  path  repo  commit  note
# `note` is the last field and may contain spaces. `path` is relative to the clone;
# `.` means the repository root IS the subject.
#
# ── SET ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# dev      designed against. A rule may be derived from what these projects show.
# holdout  DESIGN-BLIND. Measured, never used to derive a rule. When it surfaces a
#          defect class, the shape is reproduced SYNTHETICALLY under cases/ and the
#          rule is designed from that -- never from the held-out site itself.
#          A change that closes the dev/holdout gap is suspicious rather than good:
#          that gap IS the generalisation cost, and a fix that erases it usually
#          means the dev set leaked.
#
# ── CHOOSING THEM ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Choose them so that between them they exercise every construct the engine claims
# to handle, and so that the two sets are not the same program twice. A corpus of five
# ASP.NET web applications reports excellent coverage of a subset; the parser's own
# stratified audit is the precedent for caring about this -- its `modern-app` stratum
# had 547 files and 67 kind disagreements while `framework-bcl` had 2,217 and 1,620.
#
# The DEV set is weighted towards what a client-only engine can actually resolve:
# library code whose calls are mostly into its own source. The HOLDOUT set
# should deliberately include projects whose dominant idiom the dev set does NOT
# have -- operator-and-conversion-heavy code, expression trees, `dynamic` -- because
# a held-out set that only repeats the dev set's shapes measures nothing.
#
# ── PATH: why the project subdirectory, not the repository root ──────────────
# The IR carries `filePath` relative to the parsed root. Pointing at a repository
# root that holds src/, test/, benchmarks/ and samples/ measures the engine against
# test doubles and benchmark harnesses as much as against the library, and the
# oracle would compile all of them as one assembly -- merging several `Program`
# classes and every `internal`. Each entry therefore names the LIBRARY subtree.
#
# ── TARGET FRAMEWORKS, CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED ───────────────────────────
# Every LIBRARY subtree below lists net8.0 among its TargetFrameworks at its pinned
# commit. Verified, not assumed: an earlier version of this comment claimed the
# whole repository did, and tracing one member disproved it in one run.
#
# THE TEST PROJECTS ARE A DIFFERENT MATTER. Several target net10.0 only. That
# does not affect the STATIC
# oracle, which parses source and compiles against reference assemblies and never
# builds a project. It does affect the RUNTIME oracle, which has to build and run
# the suite, so runtime-oracle/trace-subject.sh needs an SDK new enough for the
# subject's own tests and reports the ones it cannot build rather than silently
# producing no trace.
#
# The static oracle stays pinned to Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp 4.12.0 and
# LanguageVersion 13, matching the parser's own corpus audit so a parser figure and
# an engine figure from this repository are comparable. A subject using a C# 14
# construct parses as an error there, and a file the oracle could not read is not
# evidence about the engine in either direction -- so run-corpus.sh prints the
# oracle's own compile-error count per project next to the score, and a pin that
# drifts into that problem is visible instead of quietly lowering recall.
#
# name	set	path	repo	commit	note

